\DOC LOCAL_MUL_TAC

\TYPE {LOCAL_MUL_TAC : int -> tactic}

\SYNOPSIS
Advances an {eventually}-style machine-state goal over one inlined subroutine
call, replacing the current state variable by the next one and rewriting the
assumptions to describe the post-call state.

\DESCRIBE
The single {int} argument is the index of the new state variable to introduce:
a conclusion about state {s}*n-1* becomes a conclusion about state {s}*n*, and
the surviving assumptions are restated about {s}*n*. The conclusion's body is
unchanged; only its trailing state argument is replaced by the freshly numbered
state. Assumptions describing the old state — program counter, stack pointer,
register contents, memory contents, loaded code, and the accumulated
{MAYCHANGE} frame relating the initial state to the old one — are discharged
and replaced by corresponding assumptions about the new state, while general
side conditions not mentioning a state (non-overlapping facts, arithmetic
hypotheses, abbreviation equations) are kept untouched. Among the new
assumptions, the program-counter assumption records a new code address, one
memory-region assumption records the computed result value in place of its
earlier contents, the {MAYCHANGE} frame may gain extra components for newly
written locations, and some assumptions about registers used only to pass
arguments are dropped rather than restated.
{
      A, B(s_k) ?- P(s_k)
   =================================   LOCAL_MUL_TAC n
      A, B'(s_{k+1}) ?- P(s_{k+1})
}
\noindent where {B'} is the rewritten state description.

\COMMENTS
The observed traces show only this single kind of transformation, always on
goals whose conclusion is an {eventually} statement about a machine state with
a substantial state description among the assumptions.

\ENDDOC
